WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:03.029
Homelessness has been rising in

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:04.949
America's West Coast cities for

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:06.299
more than a decade.

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:08.249
Entire blocks of Los Angeles,

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:10.109
San Francisco and Portland

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:12.089
are occupied by tent encampments

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:14.039
plagued by violence, drug

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.078
overdoses and disease.

00:00:16.079 --> 00:00:17.879
But the problem is concentrated in a

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:19.379
handful of cities.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:20.969
While nationwide, the homeless

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:22.799
population has been shrinking for

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:23.879
a decade.

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:25.769
To figure out why some places are

00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:27.899
so much more successful than others,

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:29.759
we took a trip to Texas, where

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:31.469
the homeless population declined

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:33.389
almost 30% over the last

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:35.939
decade as it grew by more than 40%

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:36.940
in California.

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:40.469
Today, the Lone Star State counts

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:42.869
90 homeless people per 100,000

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:44.879
residents versus California,

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:46.259
where the problem is almost five

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:47.699
times as bad.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:49.589
Not only does Texas have vastly

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:51.479
different politics and policies

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:53.249
from the West Coast, but it's also

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:55.079
home to three large cities with

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:56.729
three very different approaches to

00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:57.629
homelessness.

00:00:57.630 --> 00:00:59.489
Austin, San Antonio, and

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:00.490
Houston.

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:03.069
From a privately run village of tiny

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:05.049
homes just outside Austin, to

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:06.999
a nonprofit serving San Antonio's

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:08.949
homeless with an intensive, no

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:10.419
excuses, treatment and skills

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:12.369
training program to a single

00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:14.229
centralized provider in Houston

00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:15.639
that streamlined its approach

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:17.229
quickly housing thousands of the

00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:18.699
city's homeless residents.

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:20.409
What we found in Texas was

00:01:20.410 --> 00:01:21.909
innovation.

00:01:21.910 --> 00:01:23.349
But the federal government doesn't

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:24.350
fund innovation.

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:27.369
For decades, it's committed to a

00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:29.259
one-size-fits-all approach

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:31.359
known as Housing First.

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:33.339
States like California have followed

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:35.139
suit, leaving many charities with a

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:37.239
choice to either fall in line

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:39.249
or turn down millions in

00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:41.769
federal funding and state grants.

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:42.939
The result?

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:44.829
More people living and dying

00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:46.839
on the streets as governors and big

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:48.579
city mayors promise that the much

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:50.589
awaited free permanent housing

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:51.819
is just around the corner.

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:56.089
Our first stop was the city of

00:01:56.090 --> 00:01:57.979
Austin, where progressive activism

00:01:57.980 --> 00:01:59.179
exists in the shadow of a

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:00.529
conservative statehouse.

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:03.829
It's a boom and bust town, a magnet

00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:05.359
for business and tech innovation,

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:06.799
which has learned some of Silicon

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:08.659
Valley's top performers.

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:10.549
When the ultra-rich moved in,

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:12.349
housing prices started to resemble

00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:14.209
San Francisco's and the homeless

00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:16.399
population has climbed.

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:18.229
Policy wise, Austin has a lot

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:19.482
in common with West Coast cities,

00:02:19.483 --> 00:02:21.259
which helps explain the huge

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:23.089
encampments here.

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:24.679
But Austin has an advantage that San

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:26.809
Francisco and Los Angeles don't.

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:28.489
When you walk over the city line,

00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:29.989
you're in a more typical Texas

00:02:29.990 --> 00:02:31.819
municipality, where light touch

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:33.409
regulation allows innovative

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:35.509
approaches to thrive.

00:02:35.510 --> 00:02:37.369
The outskirts of Austin are home to

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:40.129
Community First Village, a 51-acre

00:02:40.130 --> 00:02:41.929
community of tiny homes.

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:43.609
The project doesn't rely on federal

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:45.529
money and therefore isn't bound by

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:47.450
rules imposed by Washington.

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:49.819
Alan Graham, who founded Community

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:51.109
First Village, attributes

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:52.519
homelessness to the lack of a

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:54.049
supportive network of friends,

00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:55.969
family and neighbors, and seeks to

00:02:55.970 --> 00:02:57.439
rebuild that network by giving

00:02:57.440 --> 00:02:58.969
formerly homeless people the

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:00.499
opportunity to live in a community

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:01.639
again.

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:02.899
You're the best for.

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:04.249
The single greatest cause of

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:05.869
homelessness is a profound,

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:08.599
catastrophic loss of family.

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:10.459
The homes are intentionally designed

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:12.289
with large front porches within a

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:13.969
walkable community to encourage

00:03:13.970 --> 00:03:16.069
socialization among neighbors.

00:03:16.070 --> 00:03:17.689
To live here, residents have to

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:19.579
respect the law and follow rules

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:21.469
like keeping pets leashed, junk

00:03:21.470 --> 00:03:23.269
off their driveways and drug use out

00:03:23.270 --> 00:03:25.069
of the common areas, but behind

00:03:25.070 --> 00:03:27.709
closed doors, that's their business.

00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:29.389
What we always wanted is for people

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:31.009
to live the way that people want to

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:32.509
live here in the United States of

00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:34.189
America. We have an extraordinary

00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:35.359
number of freedoms.

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:36.829
We don't want people coming into our

00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:38.479
homes seeing what we do in the

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:40.369
privacy of our own homes, what you

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:41.890
do there is your biz.

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:44.599
And so we tried to create that same

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:45.529
environment.

00:03:45.530 --> 00:03:47.029
But there are three fundamental

00:03:47.030 --> 00:03:48.559
rules to live here.

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:50.659
Rule number one, above

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:52.309
all rules. Period.

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:53.389
End of story.

00:03:53.390 --> 00:03:55.729
You must pay rent.

00:03:55.730 --> 00:03:57.259
The monthly costs for one of these

00:03:57.260 --> 00:03:59.239
houses is between 240

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:01.399
and $440, depending

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:02.959
on the size and amenities which

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:04.609
residents typically pay out of their

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:06.109
monthly Social Security or

00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:07.429
disability benefits.

00:04:07.430 --> 00:04:10.279
Prior to COVID, we had a 99%

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:12.155
rent collection bill.

00:04:12.156 --> 00:04:12.651
On time?

00:04:12.652 --> 00:04:13.652
I mean, you

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:16.483
know, close. Close enough.

00:04:16.484 --> 00:04:18.349
And it turns out that

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:20.869
people that have skin in the game

00:04:20.870 --> 00:04:22.849
are bought into the game far more

00:04:22.850 --> 00:04:24.229
than people that don't have skin in

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:25.159
the game.

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:28.009
The residents we spoke with agreed.

00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:29.899
One thing that I've learned

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:31.759
since I've been out here

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:33.259
is unconditional love, which I've

00:04:33.260 --> 00:04:34.729
never had, except from my

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:35.730
grandmother who's dead for over 50
years.

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:38.509
Oh, it's still conditional.

00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:41.299
Oh. Sometimes with certain people,

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:42.300
but not with you.

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:45.049
I can tell you this now, honestly,

00:04:45.050 --> 00:04:46.129
this the most incredible place in

00:04:46.130 --> 00:04:47.569
the world. People and I listen, I

00:04:47.570 --> 00:04:48.619
sit down, talk to people.

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:50.809
They just want someone to talk to.

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:52.279
There are a lot of really broken

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:53.280
people here. If you had no one to
turn to,

00:04:54.950 --> 00:04:56.029
if you're, you know, financially
broke,

00:04:56.030 --> 00:04:58.519
bankrupt, if you have no family.

00:04:58.520 --> 00:04:59.520
What can you do at the end of the
street?

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:01.729
And he taught me that.

00:05:01.730 --> 00:05:02.839
And that's why we're here every day.

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:04.429
Because when people realize we're

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:05.539
all good people, they're all good

00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:06.349
people here.

00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:07.350
We love you all.

